(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-asthma-controllers-quiz"]={"title":"Asthma Controllers: Steroids, Leukotriene Modifiers, Mast Cell Stabilizers & Biologics Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Advair combines fluticasone with which long-acting beta-2 agonist?","choices":["Salmeterol","Formoterol","Levalbuterol","Tiotropium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Advair is salmeterol with fluticasone; the two formoterol combinations are Symbicort (with budesonide) and Dulera (with mometasone). — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"How do inhaled corticosteroids control asthma?","choices":["They inhibit the inflammatory cascade","They block leukotriene D4 receptors","They bind circulating immunoglobulin E","They relax airway smooth muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled corticosteroids inhibit the inflammatory cascade, which reduces bronchial responsiveness over the long term. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Pulmicort is the brand name of which inhaled corticosteroid?","choices":["Beclomethasone","Budesonide","Fluticasone","Flunisolide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Budesonide is sold as Pulmicort, and with formoterol it makes up Symbicort. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which metabolic side effect is shared by systemic corticosteroids and beta-2 agonists?","choices":["Hypertension","Hyperuricemia","Hyperglycemia","Hypoglycemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hyperglycemia is on both lists: the systemic corticosteroid side effects and the beta-2 agonist adverse effects. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slides 89, 71"},{"q":"In which form of asthma is montelukast described as effective?","choices":["Status asthmaticus","Aspirin-induced asthma","Eosinophilic phenotype asthma","Anaphylactic bronchospasm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Montelukast, an oral leukotriene D4 antagonist, is effective in aspirin-induced asthma. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"The anti-interleukin-5 and anti-interleukin-4 biologics have a side effect profile similar to which drug?","choices":["Montelukast","Omalizumab","Prednisone","Fluticasone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The other asthma biologics have a side effect profile similar to omalizumab (Xolair), whose list includes gastrointestinal disturbances, anaphylaxis, injection site pain and bruising. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Which effect of high-dose inhaled corticosteroids is a particular concern in children?","choices":["Red-green color loss","Convulsions","Tachycardia","Growth suppression"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. High-dose inhaled corticosteroids can suppress growth in children; they can also cause oral candidiasis and inhibit the immune system. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which acute, life-threatening reaction is listed among omalizumab's adverse effects?","choices":["Status asthmaticus","Convulsions","Hepatitis","Anaphylaxis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Omalizumab's adverse effects include anaphylaxis (about 0.1 percent), gastrointestinal disturbances, neutralizing antibodies and secondary malignancies. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Mepolizumab and reslizumab are used for which asthma phenotype?","choices":["Aspirin-induced","Exercise-induced","Eosinophilic","Viral-triggered"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The other asthma biologics (anti-interleukin-5 and anti-interleukin-4) are used for the eosinophilic phenotype, with peripheral blood eosinophils above 150 per microliter. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Besides urticaria and anaphylaxis, which local effects are listed for anti-immunoglobulin E therapy?","choices":["Injection site pain, bruising","Oral thrush and dry mouth","Tremor and tachycardia","Orange tears and sweat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anti-immunoglobulin E side effects are urticaria (0.1 percent), anaphylaxis (0.1 percent), injection site pain and bruising. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Cromolyn and nedocromil prevent asthma attacks by which action?","choices":["Blocking leukotriene receptors","Binding immunoglobulin E","Inhibiting phosphodiesterase","Stabilizing mast cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cromolyn and nedocromil remove the trigger or antigen by stabilizing mast cells; their exact mechanism is unknown and may involve interference with calcium. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Symbicort combines budesonide with which drug?","choices":["Salmeterol","Albuterol","Formoterol","Montelukast"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Symbicort is formoterol with budesonide; Dulera is formoterol with mometasone, and Advair is salmeterol with fluticasone. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A patient admitted for an asthma exacerbation has taken a systemic corticosteroid for 8 days. What does the general rule say about stopping it?","choices":["Taper the dose rather than stop abruptly","Stop it at once; no taper is needed","Switch to theophylline before stopping","Continue it indefinitely as a controller"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The general rule is to taper when the course lasts more than 5 days, because systemic corticosteroids cause adrenal suppression; a 5-day course can simply stop. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"Montelukast (Singulair) belongs to which drug class?","choices":["Lipoxygenase inhibitors","Mast cell stabilizers","Leukotriene antagonists","Inhaled corticosteroids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Montelukast is an oral leukotriene antagonist: it blocks the leukotriene D4 (cysteinyl leukotriene 1) receptor, which mediates inflammation, bronchial constriction and mucus secretion. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which side effect is listed both for high-dose inhaled corticosteroids in children and for systemic corticosteroids?","choices":["Oral candidiasis","Growth suppression","Hypertension","Steroid myopathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Growth suppression appears on both lists: high-dose inhaled corticosteroids can suppress growth in children, and it is a systemic corticosteroid side effect. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slides 89, 73"},{"q":"What should a patient starting nedocromil expect?","choices":["Relief of wheezing within minutes","Plasma level checks for toxicity","Orange discoloration of the urine","Up to 4 weeks of regular use to work"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cromolyn and nedocromil may take up to 4 weeks to work and must be taken regularly, before antigen exposure. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 78"}]};
